## Environment

* SuperCollider version: 3.12.1
* Operating system: Kubuntu 20.04
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): all default, jackd, builtin sound card

## Steps to reproduce

Supernova does always crash if `maxNodes` and `memSize` aren't set and the required memory is exceeded in the example below.
Also, adding by 1000 synths I can't get to 4000 synths, it also crashes almost all the time.

```supercollider
Server.supernova;
s.options.maxNodes = 8000;
s.options.memSize = 2 ** 16;

SynthDef(\test, { Out.ar(0, SinOsc.ar(mul: 0.0001)) }).add;

g = ParGroup()

(
fork {
	1000.do { arg i;
		Synth(\test, target:g);
		0.001.wait;
	}
};
)
```

currently, supernova responds to notify messages differently than scsynth;
this makes several tests in TestServer_clientID_booted fail.
* supernova ignores requested clientID and sends back the first free one;
this makes setups with user-defined clientIDs impossible.
* supernova does not send back maxLogins, which is not critical; 
it would be useful to communicate maxLogins thru the server so all clients get the same info 
from the server process directly.
```
Server.supernova;
OSCFunc.trace;
s.boot;
// supernova does not send back maxLogins, which is not critical
s.quit;
// try to set to a different clientID while off
s.options.maxLogins_(4); s.clientID = 3;

// request that clientID when booting ...
s.boot; 
// and supernova ignores the requested ID and sends back the first free id 0
s.clientID; 
```

Hello,

I think I found a hard-to-pinpoint bug in supernova's node processing/allocation(?). In very specific circumstances, with the node tree comprising of a particular combination of synths and groups, supernova stops playing, prints (sometimes repeatedly)

> nova::dsp_queue_interpreter::run_item: possible lookup detected in dsp helper thread

then 

> nova::dsp_queue_interpreter::run_item: possible lookup detected in main audio thread

and it quits.
An [old thread](http://new-supercollider-mailing-lists-forums-use-these.2681727.n2.nabble.com/possible-lookup-detected-td7601972.html) also suggests this should be called _lockup_ and not _lookup_, but that's of minor importance... 

Does this happen for others? What would be the way to debug it?

(I'm on recent dev build on macOS 10.12.6; also happens on a recent build on Ubuntu 17.10)
